Prompt passes of 1024 rows instead of 128 for the Qwen3.5 hybrids on CUDA (max_rows, Round.max_rows, MAXR, Shape.total).

A 128-row prompt pass of Ornith-1.5-122B spent ~144 of ~205 ms in the routed experts: 128 rows route to nearly all
256 experts of each layer, so every pass streams most of the 66 GiB of expert weights (measured with stream-synced
timers per layer kind). With 0002's attention, the experts cost 1.12 ms a prompt row at 128 rows a pass, 0.46 at 512
and 0.30 at 1024; prefill went 633 -> 1229 -> 1534 tok/s at 8K tokens and 501 -> 922 -> 1087 at 98K. 2048 would buy
~10% more for another ~2 GiB of scratch. The forward's scratch (sized by max_rows; the attention partials are ~2 GiB),
the round tables, the image position rows (MAXR), the drafter's absorb buffers and its tap ring (2048 + max_rows rows)
grow with it; Shape.total widens to u16 (prompt shapes are never graph-keyed; verify windows stay within most_verify
and keep their graphs, so drafting and decode keep their arithmetic). The prompt cache keeps states on the 1024-row
grid (snap_grid = max_rows). Needs 0002 (the stock attention kernel would read the cache 1024 times a pass).
Verified on a GB10 with Ornith-1.5-122B MLX 4-bit + DFlash2: see README (prefill, drafted == "draft": false,
needle retrieval at depth, decode unchanged, 4 x 245K streams).

